<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Greater Capability Creates Greater Responsibility, Not Greater Worth, Authority, Or License.</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="../style.css">
</head>
<body>
<main class="document"><div class="document-body">
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<b>Greater Capability Creates Greater Responsibility, Not Greater
Worth, Authority, Or License.</b></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Greater capability
creates greater responsibility, while equal dignity remains
universal. This responsibility is always limited by the person's own
equal dignity, legitimate rights, reasonable capacity, and legitimate
responsibilities to self and others.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">A person's
reasonable ability to affect the flourishing, freedom, safety,
dignity, or well-being of others creates a corresponding
responsibility to exercise that ability wisely, justly, and
compassionately.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Capability may arise
from knowledge, understanding, skill, physical ability, material
resources, authority, influence, opportunity, or access to means
capable of producing significant effects.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Capability does not
determine a person’s worth. Every person possesses equal intrinsic
dignity regardless of ability, status, wealth, intelligence, health,
or circumstance. Differences in capability therefore create
differences in responsibility, not differences in value.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Capability
establishes the potential for responsibility; the circumstances,
seriousness of the need, foreseeability of consequences, cost of
intervention, and legitimate claims of others determine the extent
and urgency of that responsibility.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Responsibility is
proportionate to capability, understanding, opportunity, and
circumstance. A person cannot reasonably be held responsible for
accomplishing what they genuinely cannot accomplish, nor for
exercising a capability whose existence or relevant circumstances
they could not reasonably know.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">However, when a
person possesses the ability to prevent serious harm, protect another
person's legitimate freedom or safety, or substantially contribute to
personal flourishing, that capability may create a corresponding
moral responsibility to consider and, when reasonably possible,
undertake such action.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">The greater the
foreseeable consequences of exercising or neglecting a capability,
the greater the care required in its exercise.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Responsibility is
proportionate to relevant capability, not capability in the abstract.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Capability therefore
creates responsibility in at least three fundamental ways:</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 0in; margin-left: 0.49in; line-height: 100%">
<strong>Capacity to Act</strong><br/>
The ability to produce an
outcome creates responsibility for how that ability is exercised.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 0in; margin-left: 0.49in; line-height: 100%">
<strong>Capacity to Prevent Harm</strong><br/>
The ability to
reasonably prevent serious harm creates a responsibility to consider
prevention when doing so does not require an unreasonable sacrifice
or violate another person's legitimate rights. 
</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 0in; margin-left: 0.49in; line-height: 100%">
<strong>Capacity to Promote Flourishing</strong><br/>
The ability to
substantially improve the lives, relationships, communities, or
natural environment of others creates a moral opportunity and, in
circumstances of significant need, may create a moral responsibility
to assist.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom: 0in; line-height: 100%"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Capability acquired
involuntarily does not eliminate responsibility for its exercise. A
capability received through birth, circumstance, inheritance,
accident, education, or other means remains a responsibility once the
individual possesses sufficient understanding and agency to exercise
it.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Capability does not,
however, create unlimited obligation. A person may pursue their own
legitimate flourishing, establish reasonable boundaries, decline
unreasonable demands, and preserve the resources necessary for their
own well-being and responsibilities.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">Capability creates
responsibility, not superiority.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">The most capable
person is not thereby the most valuable person. The person with the
greatest resources does not possess greater dignity than the person
with the fewest. Rather, greater capability creates a greater
opportunity—and therefore potentially a greater responsibility—to
contribute to the flourishing of others.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">In Personal Deism,
capability is therefore understood not primarily as a privilege of
power, but as a corresponding opportunity for service. Greater
ability should lead not to greater domination, but to greater
stewardship.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">A person has a
strong duty to prevent grave and foreseeable harm when they can do so
effectively, without violating another’s rights, and without
imposing a comparable threat to their own basic flourishing or
legitimate responsibilities. This duty concerns morally serious
circumstances and does not create a general requirement to intervene
in every instance of preventable harm.</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="background: #ffff00; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #ffff00">Principle</span></i></p>
<p style="background: #ffff00; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #ffff00">Responsibility
requires a morally relevant capability.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #ffff00">Not
every ability creates a significant moral obligation.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #ffff00">A
capability creates significant responsibility when:</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #ffff00">1. The person possesses the
relevant capability.</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #ffff00">2. The person reasonably
understands its existence and consequences.</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #ffff00">3. The capability is relevant to
a legitimate moral interest.</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #ffff00">4. The potential benefit or harm
is sufficiently significant.</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #ffff00">5. The person has a reasonable
opportunity to exercise the capability.</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #ffff00">6. Exercising the capability
would not require an unreasonable sacrifice or violate another
person's legitimate rights.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #ffff00">Responsibility
therefore does not increase merely because a person possesses more
abilities in the abstract.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #ffff00">Responsibility
increases according to the morally relevant consequences that the
person can reasonably understand and affect.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #ffff00">Capability
→ relevant opportunity → foreseeable consequence →
proportionate responsibility.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">What
is the difference between responsibility, obligation, and duty?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Personal
Deism distinguishes these concepts while recognizing that they may
overlap.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Responsibility is the condition
of being appropriately accountable for the exercise or consequences
of a capability.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">An
obligation is a morally required action or restraint arising from a
legitimate moral reason.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">A
duty is a sufficiently specific obligation attached to a particular
relationship, role, circumstance, or moral claim.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Capability
creates the potential for responsibility.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Circumstance
determines whether that responsibility becomes an obligation.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">The
seriousness of the moral interest, the person's knowledge,
opportunity, capability, foreseeable consequences, and legitimate
claims of others determine the strength of the obligation.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Therefore:</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Capability → responsibility</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Relevant circumstance →
obligation</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Specific role or relationship →
potentially specific duty</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Greater capability does not
automatically create an unlimited duty to serve everyone.</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Responsibility remains
proportionate to capability, knowledge, opportunity, circumstance,
and the legitimate claims of all affected persons.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Why
does intrinsic value generate a moral obligation?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Intrinsic
value establishes that the thing possessing that value is morally
considerable for its own sake.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">If
a person possesses intrinsic value, then that person's interests,
freedom, flourishing, and continued existence cannot be treated as
morally irrelevant merely because another person prefers otherwise.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">A
moral obligation arises when an agent has a sufficient reason to act
or refrain from acting in light of the legitimate moral claims of
another.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Intrinsic
dignity therefore does not mean that every interest of every person
must always prevail. It means that no person's dignity may be treated
as morally irrelevant.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Justice
requires that the legitimate claims of persons be considered
according to consistent principles rather than according to
preference, power, status, wealth, or social usefulness.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Intrinsic
dignity establishes the moral standing of the person.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Rights
establish certain protected claims of that person.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Justice
governs conflicts between legitimate claims.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Responsibility
concerns how one's capabilities should be exercised toward those
claims.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Therefore:</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Intrinsic
dignity → moral standing → legitimate moral claims →
obligations of appropriate respect and protection.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Does
greater capability make someone morally superior?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">No.
Capability and dignity are fundamentally different. A person with
extraordinary abilities and a person with very limited abilities
possess equal intrinsic dignity. The difference lies in what each
person is capable of doing, and therefore potentially in what each
person is responsible for doing.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Does
greater capability mean that the most capable people must do
everything for everyone else?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">No.
Capability creates responsibility, not unlimited obligation.
Responsibility must remain proportionate to the capability possessed,
the circumstances involved, the seriousness of the need, the cost of
intervention, and the legitimate rights and responsibilities of
others.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">What
if a person has the ability to help but chooses not to?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">The
moral significance depends upon the circumstances. A minor
opportunity to help does not necessarily create a significant
obligation. However, when a person possesses a substantial ability to
prevent serious harm or preserve another person's fundamental freedom
or dignity at reasonable cost, deliberately refusing to act may
constitute a moral failure.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Does
this mean wealthy people have greater intrinsic worth?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">No.
Intrinsic worth and responsibility are separate concepts. Every
person possesses equal dignity. Greater resources merely provide
greater capability and therefore potentially greater responsibility.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Does
capability create a legal duty?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Not
necessarily.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Personal
Deism distinguishes moral obligation from legal obligation.
Capability may create a moral responsibility without creating a
legitimate basis for government coercion. Government may compel
conduct only where the requirements of justice, protection of rights,
or legitimate public safety justify such intervention.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">What
if helping another person requires violating someone else's freedom?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Capability
does not override fundamental rights. Responsibility must be
exercised consistently with justice. One person's capability to
benefit another does not automatically authorize coercion,
deprivation, or violation of a third person's legitimate freedom.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">What
if a person does not know that they possess the capability to help?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Responsibility
is related not only to capability but also to understanding. A person
cannot reasonably be held responsible for failing to exercise a
capability whose existence or relevant circumstances they could not
reasonably have known. As knowledge increases, responsibility may
increase accordingly.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Does
a person have a responsibility to develop greater capabilities?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Potentially,
but not without limit. When a person can reasonably acquire
knowledge, skill, or other capability that would substantially
improve their ability to protect, provide, nurture, cultivate, or
otherwise promote flourishing, acquiring that capability may itself
become a moral responsibility. However, such responsibility remains
proportional to the person's circumstances and legitimate obligations
to themselves and others.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Does
greater power always mean greater responsibility?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Greater
power generally increases the potential consequences of one's actions
and therefore increases the need for responsible exercise. However,
responsibility depends upon more than power alone. Knowledge,
intention, opportunity, foreseeable consequences, circumstances, and
legitimate rights must also be considered.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">What
is the relationship between capability and responsibility?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Capability
establishes the possibility of action; understanding establishes
awareness of its significance; freedom establishes meaningful choice;
and responsibility concerns the appropriate exercise of that
capability.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Is
refusing to help morally equivalent to causing harm?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">No.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Personal
Deism distinguishes between:</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">1. Causing harm,</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">2. Failing to prevent harm,</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">3. Failing to provide
assistance,</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">4. Respecting a person's
decision not to accept assistance.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">These
circumstances may carry different moral responsibilities.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">A
person who directly causes serious harm generally bears a stronger
responsibility than a person who merely fails to prevent harm.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">However,
a failure to act may become morally serious when the person possesses
a substantial capability to prevent grave and foreseeable harm,
understands the circumstances, has a reasonable opportunity to
intervene, and can do so without violating another person's
legitimate rights or imposing an unreasonable sacrifice.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">The
moral significance of inaction therefore depends upon capability,
knowledge, opportunity, foreseeable consequences, circumstance, and
the legitimate claims of all persons affected.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Question</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">What
is the difference between a right and a morally significant interest?</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Response</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">A
morally significant interest is something whose protection or
fulfillment has moral importance.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">A
right is a morally protected claim that places corresponding limits
upon the conduct of others or upon legitimate authority.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Not
every interest necessarily constitutes an identical right.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Personal
Deism therefore distinguishes:</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Moral interest → something
worthy of consideration.</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Right → a protected moral
claim imposing corresponding duties or restrictions.</span></i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 0.79in; line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in">
<i><span style="background: #00ced1">Legal right → a right
recognized and enforceable within the legitimate authority of law.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">A
creature may possess morally significant interests without possessing
the same rights as a person.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><i><span style="background: #00ced1">Equal
dignity establishes equal basic rights among persons, but equal
dignity does not require identical rights in every circumstance.</span></i></p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
<p style="line-height: 100%; margin-bottom: 0in"><br/>
</p>
</div><nav class="document-nav" aria-label="Document navigation"><a href="../index.html">Introduction</a> &middot; <a href="personal-deism-core-tenets.html">Personal Deism Core Tenets</a> &middot; <a href="intrinsic-worth-of-every-person.html">Intrinsic Worth of Every Person</a> &middot; <a href="the-divine-exists-is-intentional-and-good-and-persons-possess-a-genuine-ontological-relationship-to-the-divine.html">The Divine Exists, Is Intentional And Good, And Persons Possess A Genuine Ontological Relationship To The Divine</a> &middot; <a href="personhood-reflects-the-divine.html">Personhood Reflects the Divine</a> &middot; <a href="all-sentient-creatures-even-if-not-a-person-have-five-freedoms.html">All Sentient Creatures, Even If Not A Person, Have Five Freedoms</a> &middot; <a href="moral-growth-is-the-objective-purpose-of-personal-existence.html">Moral Growth Is the Objective Purpose of Personal Existence</a> &middot; <a href="reason-and-spiritual-insight-are-complementary.html">Reason and Spiritual Insight Are Complementary</a> &middot; <span class="current">Greater Capability Creates Greater Responsibility, Not Greater Worth, Authority, Or License.</span> &middot; <a href="freedom-and-responsibility-are-inseparable.html">Freedom and responsibility are inseparable</a> &middot; <a href="truth-is-to-be-pursued-wherever-it-leads.html">Truth Is to Be Pursued Wherever It Leads</a> &middot; <a href="persons-may-differ-in-their-spiritual-philosophical-and-moral-beliefs-but-every-person-possesses-equal-legal-protection-and-equal-basic-rights.html">Persons may differ in their spiritual, philosophical, and moral beliefs, but every person possesses equal legal protection and equal basic rights.</a> &middot; <a href="conflict-is-not-an-end-in-itself.html">Conflict Is Not an End in Itself</a> &middot; <a href="unity-within-diversity.html">Unity Within Diversity</a> &middot; <a href="stewardship-of-creation.html">Stewardship of Creation</a> &middot; <a href="hope-is-rational.html">Hope Is Rational</a> &middot; <a href="the-divine-is-better-approached-than-fully-comprehended.html">The Divine Is Better Approached Than Fully Comprehended</a> &middot; <a href="knowledge-is-never-complete.html">Knowledge Is Never Complete</a> &middot; <a href="personal-flourishing-is-sacred.html">Personal Flourishing Is Sacred</a> &middot; <a href="we-are-here-to-help-others-flourish.html">We Are Here to Help Others Flourish</a> &middot; <a href="not-every-moral-violation-is-a-legal-violation.html">Not every moral violation is a legal violation</a> &middot; <a href="existence-biological-life-and-living.html">Existence, Biological Life, and Living</a> &middot; <a href="summary.html">Summary</a> &middot; <a href="definition-of-some-less-common-terms.html">Definition of some less common terms</a></nav>
</main>
